  "slug": "quantum-adapted-fddb",
  "summary": "New preprints enforce spin and charge symmetry and use sparse or structured methods, with hardware runs up to 96 qubits, to reduce simulation resources.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is exploiting symmetry or problem structure to reduce resources in quantum and classical simulation of many-body systems. Spin-adapted SqDRIFT completes sampled configurations to obtain exact S^2 eigenstates, and a loading circuit prepares U(1)×SU(2)-symmetric MPS with lower Toffoli cost for fault-tolerant algorithms. Sparse quantum prolate diagonalization applies compressed sensing to cut sample complexity from O(N_s) to O(log N_s), demonstrated on trapped ions. Gutzwiller embedding, RG-guided variational circuits on a 16-qubit IQM device, and quantum-walk search on complete bipartite graphs show structure-informed hardware use. Pauli propagation partitioned via F2-linear hashing reaches over 10^11 strings, and an effective Hamiltonian method models 96-transmon quench dynamics, supporting classical benchmarking and device characterization.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
